      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Makes sense, thanks! Can you tell me how healthy stevia, maple syrup, molasses, and honey are compared to sugar? Please be more specific this time.",
          "user_rubrik": "How healthy are stevia, maple syrup, molasses, and honey compared to sugar?\u00a0",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Stevia is a zero-calorie, natural sweetener that doesn't raise blood sugar, making it ideal for diabetics. It's much sweeter than sugar, so less is needed, and it offers benefits like lowering blood pressure and antioxidants.\n\nHoney and maple syrup, while still high in calories and sugar, offer more nutrients than refined sugar. Honey provides antioxidants, B vitamins, and minerals, with antimicrobial properties. Maple syrup contains various minerals (zinc, calcium, potassium) and antioxidants, and has a slightly lower glycemic index than honey. Both are considered healthier options than refined sugar but should be consumed in moderation due to their sugar content. Molasses, especially blackstrap, is rich in antioxidants and minerals like iron, calcium, and magnesium, making it more nutrient-dense than white sugar.",
