      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019m 25 years old. I\u2019ve also been eating a lot of white rice\u2014does it have much calcium or other nutrients?",
          "user_rubrik": "How much calories do brown rice, quinoa, cauliflower rice, and black rice have?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "For a 25-year-old woman your calcium target is about 1,000 mg/day (food + supplements). White rice provides very little calcium, so relying on it won\u2019t help you reach that goal. It\u2019s mainly a source of carbs and \u2014 if enriched \u2014 some B vitamins; brown rice gives more fiber and minerals than white rice.  Practical swaps and additions to boost calcium at lunch: - Stir tofu or tempeh into rice bowls (firm tofu can be high in calcium if prepared with calcium sulfate).   - Top bowls with canned salmon or sardines (with bones), sesame seeds or tahini, chopped almonds, or a sprinkle of cheese.   - Use leafy greens (kale, bok choy, spinach) in stir-fries or salads alongside rice.   - Replace one cup of white rice occasionally with quinoa for more protein and minerals.  Remember: 1 cup milk or fortified plant milk is roughly ~300 mg calcium, and vitamin D helps absorption. If you can\u2019t meet needs from food, discuss supplements with your clinician.",
