          "text": "short answer : they differ a lot \u2014 in calories, carbs, baking behavior and flavor. - sugar ( sucrose ) : high \u2011 calorie, raises blood glucose, provides bulk / texture in baking. - honey : still high in calories and carbs ( higher fructose \u2192 sweeter ). liquid \u2014 reduce other liquids or add 1 tbsp flour per 1 / 4 cup honey ; browns faster ( lower oven 25\u00b0f ). good flavor but not low \u2011 carb. - maple syrup : similar carbs / calories to sugar ( mostly sucrose ). liquid \u2014 same liquid / fluency tips as honey ; cup \u2011 for \u2011 cup sweetness is close. adds maple flavor. - molasses : caloric but nutrient \u2011 dense relative to sugar ( iron, minerals ) and very flavorful \u2014 use sparingly for strong flavor. also liquid \u2014 adjust liquids / flour. - stevia ( with erythritol ) : non \u2011 nutritive ( near zero calories ) and low \u2011 carb. pure stevia is extremely concentrated and can taste bitter ; blends with erythritol avoid cooling and match sugar better. a common diy target is ~ 1 %",
