          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "short answer : it depends what you mean by \u201c healthy. \u201d here \u2019 s a quick comparison and practical tips. - stevia ( and monk fruit ) : virtually zero calories, no meaningful blood \u2011 sugar rise, so best if you want lower carbs. can have an aftertaste and different baking behavior ; often blended with bulking sweeteners. - maple syrup & honey : natural, contain small amounts of minerals and antioxidants, but are still sugars with similar calories and can raise blood glucose. honey may have slightly higher fructose ; both are about as calorie \u2011 dense as table sugar. - molasses ( especially blackstrap ) : still sugary but richer in minerals ( iron, calcium, magnesium ). nutrient content is meaningful in small part, but it \u2019 s not a calcium solution by itself. - table sugar : calorie \u2011 dense, no nutrients. practical tips : for lower carbs, prefer stevia / monk fruit or erythritol blends ; for taste / nutrition swaps, use a little blackstrap molasses or raw honey / maple sparingly. or make low \u2011 sugar jam ( berries + chia or a bit of",
